Lesson 3: Variables & Python Syntax
What is a Variable?

A variable is a named location in memory used to store data.

Think of it as a label attached to a value.

name  ─────► "Suman"

age   ─────► 22

salary─────► 25000

Creating Variables

Unlike Java, you don't declare the data type.

Python
name = "Suman"
age = 22
height = 5.8
Java
String name = "Suman";
int age = 22;
double height = 5.8;

Python automatically determines the data type.

Variable Assignment
name = "Alex"

print(name)

Output

Alex
Multiple Variables
name = "Alex"
age = 22
city = "Kolkata"

print(name)
print(age)
print(city)

Output

Alex
22
Kolkata

Multiple Assignment
Method 1
x, y, z = 10, 20, 30

print(x)
print(y)
print(z)

Output
10
20
30

Method 2
a = b = c = 100

print(a)
print(b)
print(c)

Output
100
100
100

Swapping Variables

One of Python's coolest features.

x = 10
y = 20

x, y = y, x

print(x)
print(y)

Output

20
10

In Java, you'd usually need a temporary variable.

Dynamic Typing

Python variables can change their type.

x = 10

print(x)

x = "Hello"

print(x)

Output
10
Hello

Check the Data Type
Use the type() function.

name = "Suman"

print(type(name))

Output

<class 'str'>

More examples:

print(type(100))
print(type(3.14))
print(type(True))

Output
<class 'int'>
<class 'float'>
<class 'bool'>

Variable Naming Rules
Valid
student_name = "Alex"

totalMarks = 90

age2 = 25

_price = 100
Invalid
2age = 20

Cannot start with a number.

my-name = "Alex"

Hyphens are not allowed in variable names.

class = 10

Keywords cannot be used as variable names.

Naming Conventions

Python follows snake_case.

student_name = "Alex"

total_marks = 90

first_name = "John"

Java commonly uses camelCase.

studentName
totalMarks
firstName
Keywords

Keywords are reserved words with special meanings.

Examples:

if
else
for
while
class
return
import
True
False
None

To see all Python keywords:

import keyword

print(keyword.kwlist)
Constants

Python doesn't have true constants, but by convention we use UPPER_CASE.

PI = 3.14159

MAX_USERS = 100

This tells other developers that the value shouldn't be changed.

Memory Reference

Variables point to objects in memory.

a = 10

b = a

print(a)
print(b)

Both variables refer to the same integer object.

Delete a Variable
name = "Suman"

del name

Trying to use it afterwards:

print(name)

Produces:

NameError: name 'name' is not defined
Printing Variables
name = "Alex"
age = 22

print(name)
print(age)

Print on one line:

print(name, age)

Output

Alex 22
